'''Bowler Cat''' is a [[:Category:Rare Cats|Rare Cat]] that can be unlocked when beating the [[:Category:Advent Stages|Advent Stage]] [[Hunt for the Xenobeast]] appearing on the '''7th''', '''8th''', '''21st''' and '''22nd''' days of every month. He was added in [[Version 14.1 Update|Version 14.1]].

{{Cat Info
|ID = 780
|rarity = RR
|name1 = Bowler Cat
|name2 = Dodgeballer Cat
}}

==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Dodgeballer Cat''' at level 10.

==Strategy/Usage==
While Bowler Cat looks quite good at first glance thanks to his decent range, 100% chance to slow two threatening traits with decent uptime, and other relevant abilities, he has several flaws that hold him back from being great. His most major flaw is accessibility. While his toolkit would seemingly make him useful against [[Kappy Jr.]] and [[Kappy Kawano]], especially in their final stages where they're paired with [[:Category:Relic Enemies|Relic Enemies]], the fact that he's gated behind [[Xenobeast Bunaglios|Xenobeast Bunaglios']] Great Advent likely means that you'll have cleared Kappy Jr.'s stages before getting Bowler Cat, and Kappy Kawano outranges Bowler Cat which makes his usefulness there very questionable.

His [[Sage Slayer]] might seem useful considering the limited alternatives, but the only [[:Category:Sage Enemies|Sages]] that currently fall within his target traits are [[Dogenstein]], [[Diabrollow]] and [[Marquis Clionius]], none of which really demand being slowed. In fact, Diabrollow can be outright detrimental to use Bowler Cat on, and since Marquis Clionius has far higher range, Slow won't impede it much. Additionally, his base health is rather low, meaning even with higher effective stats from Sage Slayer he can still perish quickly in [[Zero Legends]] due to the sheer damage of the enemies there.

[[Immune to Curse|Immunity to Curse]] sounds good on paper, but Bowler Cat's health is too low to tank many hits from most [[:Category:Enemies with Curse ability|cursing enemies]]. While the [[Relic Doge Base (Enemy Base)|Relic Doge Base]] does have rather low attack power, both of the stages that pair it with Traitless enemies ([[A Pile of Guts]] and [[Stylist Mononoke]]) have [[Bore Jr.]] whose Mini-Waves will absolutely shred any stack of Bowler Cats if they connect, unless Bowler Slows Bore Jr. long enough for other attackers to intervene. As for Traitless enemies that can curse, Bowler Cat barely outranges [[Cafeteria Cat (Enemy)|Cafeteria Cat]]'s Omni Strike, but Cafeteria Cat is Slow Immune and not that hard to stall. Bowler Cat's poor uptime combined with a decently long recharge also means he won't see much use stalling [[Master Uril (Enemy)|Master Uril]] in Heavenly Tower, as Uril and the nasty Relics can steamroll him easily, rendering his Curse Immunity pointless. He can, however, be used against Uril in [[Golden Tower]], where weaker Metals replace the Relics, allowing him to be stacked and support crowd-controllers in perma-stalling Uril.

Despite his weaknesses, Bowler Cat can still be of use against powerful Traitless and Aku foes that he can outrange, such as [[R.Ost]], [[Aku Gory]], [[Condemned Peng]] and [[Rain D.]] Shield Piercing units can be optionally included since they block status effects and he has no hopes of breaking them on his own. Then again, this may not be necessary, as he competes with far more accessible specialists like [[Ancient Egg: N005 (Special Cat)|Hitman Cat]], [[Jagando Jr. (Special Cat)|Jagando]], [[Ancient Egg: N006 (Special Cat)|Fallen Bear Cat]] and [[Master Uril (Special Cat)|Esoteric Uril]], all of which have greater utility in their niche.

